WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.320
In 2009, The New Teacher Project sparked
a wave of reform when it reported that less

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.440
than 1% of teachers were rated
unsatisfactory even in school systems

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.240
with abysmal student outcomes. Urged
on by Washington, more than 30 states

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.250
adopted new teacher evaluation systems.
Good idea, but a case study in how

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.539
well-intentioned reforms can turn into
clumsy mandates. Principals in Nevada, for

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:33.059
example, now estimate they spend 150
hours – or 19 work days a year – filling out

00:00:33.059 --> 00:00:36.840
required paperwork that simply
rehashes what they'd already observed in

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.890
classrooms, recorded on paper, and
discussed with teachers. It wastes time,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.940
and that's a recipe for chasing away the
kind of great school leaders we need. And

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.350
for all of the paper shuffling, still
just one-to-two percent of teachers are

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.699
rated unsatisfactory. Look, teachers
absolutely need to be evaluated more

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:58.590
rigorously. The way to do this, though, is
not by imposing paper burdens. It's by

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:03.329
giving school leaders more authority,
leeway, and autonomy, and then holding

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.760
them responsible for what they do with
it. how do you think we should hold

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.000
How do you think we should hold educators accountable?

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.340
Let us know in your comments. Also, let us know what

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.240
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.810
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

